#!/usr/bin/env bash

lordhavemercy -f --verbose << "EOF"
All relative paths below are relative to the repo / vault root.

Refactor the existing functionality as follows:

1. Refactor the existing code to separate code from data & share functionality.
    Make sure there's no remaining shared functionality in root/src.
    If there is, then factor it out and put it in yhwh/src/lib
    Also put yhwh-3d into yhwh/src/yhwh-three & rename 3d to three throughout.
    Also put yhwh-obsidian into yhwh/src/yhwh-obsidian
    Also put yhwh into yhwh/src/yhwh-default
    Any shared functionality between the default and three and obsidian
        versions should go in yhwh/src/lib.
    Rename bin/yhwh to bin/yhwh-default in preparation for the stuff below.
    Also fix up the top level Makefile and any subdirectory Makefiles
        to make sure everything works as expected. We should be able
        to type 'make' from the repo root to build and open the app.
        Typing 'make' should do that by invoking ONLY stuff that lives in yhwh/
    The make target 'make install' should install the app into the bin/
        directory in the repo root in the form of N+1 scripts where N
        is the number of skins, currently 3.
    So 'make install' should install bin/yhwh{,-default,-three,obsidian}
        as three executables that don't point to the source code anymore,
        to the extent that that's possible in nodejs. If it's not possible,
        then just make it work. But here's what I'm going for:
    The 'make install' target should emulate installing the app "as it will
        be after we ship it." So it's like 'pip install' in python.
    The 'make develop' target should emulate installing the app "in
        editable mode" So it's like 'pip install -e .' in python.
    If nodejs can do anything remotely like that, then do that.
    If it can't, then just make whichever of the two make targets more
        closely resembles what you implemented, and make the other target
        exist but be a stub that says "Not implemented yet" plus the
        reasons. If you can't implement both, then add whichever one
        you didn't do yet to the COVENANT kanban board.
    Back to the refactor.
    Ok so...
    Any interface text that we might want to change should be kept
        in a separate location from the js code, let's put it in
        yhwh/etc since it's static config that we're gonna be editing
        to change the interface's text behavior.
    What to put in yhwh/etc:
        Any interface config variables that determine things like the geometry
            (width, height) of various components should be in
            yhwh/etc/ in a file or file.d called geometry or workspace or ui
            or whatever is most standard in the electron space.
        Emulate what a linux or unix distro would do whenever possible.
        I want the messy implementation code and stuff that would scare
        a human to be in yhwh/src, but I want the human readable and machine
        readable config to be in yhwh/etc, same as how unix does.
        Any internal support scripts should be in yhwh/bin.
        Any internal importable libraries should be in yhwh/lib.
        Any build outputs should be put in yhwh/tmp or yhwh/var/lib/
        in a separate location from the js code, let's put it in
        yhwh/var since it's dynamic config that we're gonna be editing
        to change the interface's text behavior.

- Move amr to doc/bin
EOF
